  "concept": {
    "name": "lens-medium",
    "lens": "medium",
    "fragment": "LENS \"medium\": the artwork's MATERIAL AND MARKS become the island's physical character. Start from ANALYSIS.medium and ANALYSIS.palette, not from the subject. The ground is made of the marks: single-stroke ink becomes ribbon land and sweeping arcs of path with void where the paper is empty (shaper \"ribbon\", or \"spiral\" for a curl); airbrush gradients become soft radial terraces fading outward (shaper \"radial\"); fauvist or knife strokes become a patchwork of flat colour blocks at different heights (shaper \"patchwork\"); charcoal smudge becomes soft-edged dark masses with pale mist lying in the hollows (shaper \"mound\" or \"basin\"); pencil hatching or scratchy ink becomes angular slabs split by thin seams (shaper \"shards\"); vector doodles become dense small ornament on a tidy mirrored plan (shaper \"symmetric\"). Choose shaper params from the marks themselves (how many strokes, how wide, which way they sweep, how many blocks). Palette, surface_mix and cliff_treatment follow the medium (ink: paper-pale sand and ink-dark cliffs; airbrush: rings of surfaces fading by value; fauvist: saturated patches; charcoal: soft grey-green, mossy or soft cliffs). New props are the marks made solid: a brush flick as a curved blade of stone, a smudge as a low soft boulder, a doodle as a ring of beads, a knife stroke as a flat tilted slab. The figure's pose comes from how it is drawn (a single continuous stroke, a stack of colour patches), and it may be a monument of the marks rather than a living creature."
  }
